    "paragraphHighlights": [
      "The main brand guardrails guide tells you what layers to build. This post shows you exactly what the document looks like — a real, fillable AI Brand Rulebook template covering allowed use cases, red lines, sources of truth, tone rules, and escalation logic.",
      "This post is a companion to the brand guardrails guide — it shows you what the governance document actually looks like, filled in for a realistic B2B SaaS marketing org.",
      "The rulebook has five sections: operating perimeter, red lines, sources of truth, tone & format rules, and escalation protocol — each maps directly to a layer of the guardrail framework.",
      "Red lines are not vague principles — they are specific claim types (e.g., 'no pricing not on the current rate card') that go into your system prompt and banned-term list verbatim.",
      "Sources of truth must be versioned and dated — a stale product spec fed to a RAG system is a hallucination waiting to happen.",
      "Use this template as-is for a first draft, then adapt the red lines and sources of truth to your specific product category and compliance requirements.",
      "How the rulebook maps to your guardrail layers",
      "Section 2: Red lines — what the AI must never say",
      "Section 5: Escalation and human review protocol",
      "The most common failure point when teams try to implement AI guardrails isn't the technology — it's that the governance document never gets written. Engineers can't build a RAG corpus, configure validators, or tune system prompts until someone has decided what the AI is actually allowed to say. That decision lives in the AI Brand Rulebook, and it belongs to marketing and legal, not engineering."
    ],
