    "paragraphHighlights": [
      "A 2026 Google Research + UMass paper (arXiv:2601.05588) proves the dual-encoder capacity ceiling is linear in corpus size — ≈464,000 dims for 1M documents. Autoregressive LLM rankers need just 512. Here's the theorem, the SToICaL loss that makes generative ranking actually work, and what it means for your RAG stack.",
      "The paper (arXiv:2601.05588, Google Research + UMass) proves a strict capacity separation: dual encoders need embedding dimension growing linearly with corpus size to rank all permutations, while autoregressive LLM rankers can do it with constant hidden dimension.",
      "Three architectures, one map: dual encoders (two towers, pre-computed vectors, fast but limited), cross encoders (joint forward pass, accurate but O(N) at query time), autoregressive rankers (LLM generates docIDs token-by-token with beam search).",
      "Vanilla next-token prediction is rank-agnostic and wastes the rank information in your training data — it gets top-1 roughly right but ranking beyond top-1 is noisy.",
      "SToICaL (Simple Token-Item Calibrated Loss) fixes this with two tricks: item-level reweighting by 1/rᵅ, and trie-based token-level target distributions that marginalize rank information across valid docID continuations.",
      "Head-to-head on WordNet, ARR trained with SToICaL matches cross-encoder ranking quality while significantly beating dual encoders — the first rigorous evidence that generative retrieval has both theoretical and practical ceiling advantages.",
      "What are dual encoders, cross encoders, and autoregressive ranking?",
      "What does \"expressive capacity\" mean for ranking?",
      "Why do dual encoders hit a hard ceiling?",
      "Why can autoregressive rankers rank with constant dimension?"
    ],
