    "paragraphHighlights": [
      "Research-backed frameworks for structuring content that gets cited by generative engines. What the studies actually found.",
      "1The Original GEO Paper: What Princeton & IIT Delhi Actually Found",
      "2How to Dominate AI Search: The First Comparative Study of GEO Across All Major Engines",
      "3How to Use Google's LangExtract Library to Improve Your GEO",
      "4How the Architecture of Embedding Models Determines Whether AI Retrieves Your Content",
      "5FeatGEO: Why the Original 9 GEO Tactics Are Failing on Modern AI Engines",
      "A 2026 paper from Liu and Xu re-runs the Princeton GEO experiments on GPT-4o-mini, Gemini 2.5 Flash, and Qwen-plus — and finds most of the original 9 heuristics now decrease citation visibility. Their replacement, FeatGEO, treats GEO as feature-level multi-objective optimization.",
      "The original 9 GEO heuristics from the Princeton paper (keyword stuffing, authoritative tone, even adding citations) now decrease citation visibility on modern AI engines — by up to 4 percentage points versus the unmodified baseline (Gemini drops from 8.89% to 4.62%).",
      "Token-level rewriting saturates: it helps weak human-written pages with structural gaps, but hurts already-fluent LLM-generated content because it disrupts the natural patterns generative engines prefer to cite.",
      "FeatGEO replaces token edits with feature-level optimization — abstracting pages into 13 structural, content, and linguistic features, then running NSGA-II evolutionary search to find Pareto-optimal solutions."
    ],
