    "paragraphHighlights": [
      "Why AI engines reward flywheel content models over traditional funnels — and how to build a self-sustaining content system that compounds AI visibility through customer advocacy.",
      "AI engines cite earned media (third-party reviews, analyst reports, community content) 70–90% of the time — what others say about you matters more than what you publish.",
      "Traditional funnels fail AI search because they end at conversion; AI search rewards self-reinforcing systems where customers generate citations.",
      "The flywheel: product experience → delighted customers → customer advocacy → earned media → AI citations → new customers discovering your brand.",
      "Five programs to build: customer interviews, case studies, community forums, review campaigns, and partner co-authorship.",
      "Earned content generates AI citations, which builds trust, which generates more customers — the compounding effect is the strategic advantage.",
      "AI search doesn’t reward “more content.” It rewards more evidence.",
      "Traditional funnels optimize for traffic → leads → conversion. But generative engines optimize for justification: “Which sources can I cite to defend this recommendation?”",
      "Large-scale comparative research across ChatGPT, Claude, Perplexity, and Gemini shows a strong bias toward earned media—third-party coverage, independent reviews, and customer advocacy—often dominating citations compared to brand-owned and social sources. That means what others say about you can matter more than what you publish on your own site. (See: Generative Engine Optimization: How to Dominate AI Search, arXiv:2509.08919. For the full breakdown, r...",
      "So the winning model is not a funnel that ends at conversion. It’s a flywheel where customers become the engine of your visibility."
    ],
