    "paragraphHighlights": [
      "GA4 channel conditions use RE2 regex — not PCRE. Here are validated patterns for all major AI engines, common mistakes that silently break your channel definitions, and a maintenance schedule to keep coverage accurate as new AI domains appear.",
      "GA4 channel conditions use RE2 regex — not PCRE. No lookaheads, lookbehinds, or backreferences. Alternation with escaped dots covers all major AI engines.",
      "Full validated pattern: ^(chatgpt\\.com|chat\\.openai\\.com|perplexity\\.ai|claude\\.ai|gemini\\.google\\.com|copilot\\.microsoft\\.com|bing\\.com|you\\.com|meta\\.ai)$ — anchors prevent overly broad matching.",
      "Use 'matches regex' as the match type in GA4 channel conditions — selecting 'contains' treats the regex as a literal string and it will never fire.",
      "Always include both chatgpt.com and chat.openai.com; properties that only match chatgpt.com systematically miss sessions from bookmarks and older links.",
      "Schedule a quarterly review of your top 50 referral sources in Explorations to catch new AI engine domains before they corrupt your Referral channel metrics.",
      "Common mistakes that break channel definitions",
      "Writing the regex for a GA4 custom channel group sounds simple — until you realize GA4 uses RE2 syntax, not the PCRE regex most practitioners are familiar with, and that a single malformed pattern silently assigns sessions to the wrong channel with no error message.",
      "This is Step 3 in the GA4 for AI Search measurement series. It covers the validated regex patterns for all major AI engines, the RE2 syntax constraints you need to know, and a process for keeping patterns accurate as AI engines expand to new domains.",
      "GA4's channel group condition field uses RE2 — Google's regex engine that trades some advanced features for guaranteed linear-time matching. If you are used to PCRE (used in PHP, Python's re module, or most regex testers), the practical differences are:"
    ],
