    "paragraphHighlights": [
      "A data-minded take on what the report gets right, where the framing blurs, and how to validate Similarweb trends more rigorously.",
      "Graphite's analysis of 40,000 large US sites shows SEO traffic down −2.5% YoY — far below the 30–60% collapse claimed in many industry narratives.",
      "The study conflates 'SEO traffic,' 'organic traffic from Google,' and 'all search engines' — a distinction that affects interpretation.",
      "Validating Similarweb data with Pearson correlation shows two trends move together — it does not prove the absolute numbers are accurate.",
      "The decline is uneven: some site categories and sizes experience significantly larger drops than the aggregate average.",
      "'SEO is dead' is overstated, but the reshuffle is real — traffic redistribution is happening, just not uniformly or catastrophically.",
      "I keep seeing bold claims like “SEO is down 30–60%” and “search is dying because everyone moved to AI.”",
      "So I read Graphite's analysis, where they say the reality is much less dramatic: across 40,000 large US sites, organic search traffic is down only -2.5% YoY (not -25%+).",
      "I’m going to be polite but honest: I think the core direction of the article is good, but a few parts of the methodology (and wording) leave openings for confusion — especially around what “SEO traffic” means and how they validate Similarweb data using Pearson correlation.",
      "I agree with Graphite’s main point: “SEO collapsed everywhere” is likely overstated."
    ],
