    "paragraphHighlights": [
      "LLM evaluation metrics fall into distinct families — each designed to catch a different failure mode. This taxonomy maps every major metric category, explains what each measures, and shows which failure mode it's designed to catch so you can build eval suites that actually cover your risk surface.",
      "LLM eval metrics fall into six families: reference-based, model-based, human-based, safety-based, calibration-based, and task-specific — each requiring different infrastructure to compute.",
      "The most-cited metrics (BLEU, ROUGE) are reference-based and measure token overlap — they are a poor proxy for semantic quality and actively mislead teams evaluating open-ended generation.",
      "Faithfulness and groundedness are frequently confused: faithfulness checks if the answer is supported by provided context; groundedness checks if facts are externally verifiable.",
      "Safety metrics require specialized classifiers (Llama Guard, Perspective API) and adversarial test suites — they cannot be approximated by general-purpose LLM judges reliably.",
      "No single metric is sufficient: a minimum viable eval suite covers at least one metric per failure mode family — typically faithfulness + relevance + safety + instruction-following.",
      "Family 1: Reference-based metrics (and why to be careful)",
      "Family 2: Model-based metrics (LLM-as-judge)",
      "Family 4: Safety and alignment metrics",
      "Family 5: Calibration and uncertainty metrics"
    ],
