    "paragraphHighlights": [
      "How Andrej Karpathy's LLM Council uses multi-model polling, anonymous peer review, and synthesis to reduce hallucinations and improve answer quality.",
      "Andrej Karpathy's LLM Council queries multiple AI models simultaneously, has them critique each other anonymously, and synthesizes a consensus answer.",
      "Three-stage workflow: independent answers → anonymous peer review (no model knows whose answer it's critiquing) → chairman synthesis.",
      "Anonymous review prevents anchoring bias — models can't defer to a 'known authoritative' response.",
      "Works best for high-stakes, ambiguous questions where single-model bias or blind spots could produce confidently wrong answers.",
      "Tradeoff: 3–5× higher cost and latency vs a single model — reserve for decisions where quality matters more than speed.",
      "Single-model AI systems have a fundamental weakness: every LLM carries its own biases, blind spots, and failure modes. The LLM Model Council, created by Andrej Karpathy, addresses this by treating multiple models as a deliberative body—polling them, having them critique each other, and synthesizing a final answer through consensus.",
      "When to use a council vs a single model",
      "The LLM Model Council is an open-source project that queries multiple AI models simultaneously, has them review each other's responses anonymously, and then synthesizes a final answer through a designated \"Chairman\" model. The concept mirrors human decision-making structures like boards of directors, scientific peer review, or medical councils.",
      "The tool connects to models from OpenAI, Google, Anthropic, xAI, and open-source providers through OpenRouter, treating them as interchangeable endpoints. This means you can swap models in and out based on performance without changing application code."
    ],
