    "paragraphHighlights": [
      "A new paper (arXiv:2604.19516) proposes MAGEO — a four-agent GEO framework that distills successful content edits into reusable, engine-specific strategies. The result: WLV scores more than triple versus the strongest heuristic baseline on GPT-5.2 and Gemini-3 Pro, while reducing hallucinated citations.",
      "MAGEO (arXiv:2604.19516) is a four-agent GEO framework where a Preference, Planner, Editor, and Evaluator agent collaborate iteratively — and successful editing patterns are distilled into a Skill Bank for reuse across future tasks.",
      "On MSME-GEO-Bench, MAGEO achieves a Word-Level Visibility score of 4.52 on GPT-5.2 and 5.30 on Gemini-3 Pro — more than tripling the strongest single heuristic baseline (More Quotes: 1.33).",
      "Removing engine-specific preference modeling drops performance ~19% on GPT-5.2; removing the Skill Bank drops ~13% — both components contribute independently and measurably.",
      "The paper finds GEO is engine-specific by default: Gemini-3 Pro favors compact tables and URLs, GPT-5.2 prefers authority-seeking citation formatting, and Qwen-3 Max prefers didactic safety-aware prose.",
      "For SEOs: heuristic GEO checklists applied uniformly across engines are provably suboptimal — the gap between MAGEO and stacked heuristics is not closed by combining more rules.",
      "Why is the current GEO playbook structurally broken?",
      "What problem is MAGEO actually solving?",
      "How does the four-agent MAGEO system work?",
      "What is the Skill Bank and why does it change everything?"
    ],
