    "paragraphHighlights": [
      "Compare reranker options, tradeoffs, and selection criteria for production RAG.",
      "Reranking is a precision pass that re-scores retrieval results using a deeper model — improving answer quality without rebuilding embeddings or indexes.",
      "Cross-encoders jointly process query + passage and capture fine-grained alignment, negation, and subtle constraints — strong default choice.",
      "LLM rerankers use natural language reasoning to score relevance — better on complex tasks but 5–10× higher cost and latency.",
      "Use cross-encoders for most production RAG pipelines; reserve LLM rerankers for high-value, low-volume decision flows.",
      "Failure mode: applying reranking when your base retriever already delivers high-precision results — adds cost without meaningful gain.",
      "Reranking is the sharpest tool you have for improving reranking rag quality without blowing up your index or retriever complexity. This guide compares cross-encoder and LLM rerankers and shows how to pick the right one for your latency cost tradeoff.",
      "How to choose: a practical decision framework",
      "Most retrieval systems are optimized for speed and recall, not precision. That means the initial retriever tends to surface a noisy top k set: plenty of relevant chunks mixed with near-duplicates, partial matches, and outright tangents. Reranking is the precision pass that rescues answer quality by re-scoring those candidates with a deeper model.",
      "For engineers, this is the sweet spot. You get a measurable boost in retrieval quality without rebuilding embeddings or reshaping the index. It also gives you a safety valve: if your base retriever is cheap and fast, you can afford to cast a wider net and let reranking handle the cleanup."
    ],
