    "paragraphHighlights": [
      "LLM evals are systematic methods for measuring whether an AI system's outputs meet defined quality standards. This guide covers what evals are, why they're necessary, the three evaluation approaches, and what a practical eval pipeline looks like from first test case to production gate.",
      "LLM evals are repeatable, measurable tests that verify AI outputs meet quality standards across correctness, safety, relevance, and format — the AI equivalent of a test suite.",
      "Three evaluation approaches exist: automated evals (rule-based, fast, cheap), model-based evals (LLM-as-judge, scalable for open-ended outputs), and human evals (highest accuracy, not scalable).",
      "A minimal eval pipeline has four components: a test dataset, an LLM pipeline to evaluate, a scoring function, and a threshold that gates deployment.",
      "Evals are not a one-time exercise: the eval-deploy loop — evaluate, identify failures, fix, re-evaluate — is the core development cycle for any AI product shipping quality at speed.",
      "The most common eval mistake is building evals only after a quality incident. Teams that build evals before first launch catch 62% more failure modes than those that add evals reactively.",
      "Anatomy of an eval: the four components",
      "Every software team has a test suite. Every AI team needs an eval suite. The problem is that most teams building with LLMs treat evaluation as an afterthought — something to add after users complain, not before the first deployment. This guide closes that gap: what LLM evals are, why they exist, and how to build a pipeline that actually catches failures before they reach production.",
      "An LLM eval is a repeatable, measurable test that checks whether an AI system's output meets a defined quality standard for a specific input.",
      "That definition contains three load-bearing words: repeatable (you can run it again and compare), measurable (it produces a score or pass/fail signal), and defined standard (you decided in advance what \"good\" means). Remove any of these and you have informal testing, not an eval."
    ],
